112
00:13:41.834 --> 00:13:44.175
<v Daniel>inequality over a five year apprenticeship.</v>

113
00:13:44.904 --> 00:13:59.613
<v Maya>So what does this all mean? If we synthesize this deep dive, AI is obviously a phenomenal tool for output. It levels the playing field while you're actively using it. But for building long-term internalized expertise, it seems like AI only acts as a teacher if</v>

114
00:13:59.674 --> 00:14:02.816
<v Maya>you already have a deep foundation of knowledge to stand on.</v>

115
00:14:03.016 --> 00:14:17.719
<v Daniel>Yeah, and if we zoom out to the macro level, looking at the labor market and higher education, there's a huge question here. If AI is now doing all the grunt drafting work that used to be the training ground for the juniors, Where does the next generation of seniors come from? Firms and</v>

116
00:14:17.779 --> 00:14:25.743
<v Daniel>schools are going to have to decide if they should design AI workflows around actual skill formation rather than just maximizing immediate output.</v>

117
00:14:26.032 --> 00:14:40.777
<v Maya>Yeah, and thank you so much for joining us on this deep dive today. I want to leave you, the listener, with one final provocative thought to mull over. If AI completely replaces the tedious, you know, training wheels phase of our careers, we might actually have to</v>

118
00:14:40.837 --> 00:14:54.139
<v Maya>invent entirely new artificial forms of friction just to force the human brain to learn. Are we moving toward a future where doing things the hard way becomes this luxury training perk reserved only for future executives? Something to think about.</v>
